-<section id="acceptance-criteria">
-  <h2>Definition of done</h2>
-  <ul class="criteria">
-    <li id="ac-1" data-criterion>Canonical ownership is enforceable: setup-asher-skills alone owns convention pointers/modules; staffing alone owns roster pointers/modules; either owner’s reconcile leaves the other owner’s bytes unchanged.</li>
-    <li id="ac-2" data-criterion>Each harness global file contains exact on-demand triggers for routing and presentation, uses no eager import, names an absolute module path, and defines safe behavior when that module is unreadable.</li>
-    <li id="ac-3" data-criterion>All Asher-owned always-loaded global text measures at or below 140 o200k-equivalent tokens per harness; each pointer stanza is at or below 65 tokens, with raw-character gates in stdlib-only tests.</li>
-    <li id="ac-4" data-criterion>A direct leaf task that makes no routing, capability, watcher, fallback, or presentation decision does not read either deferred module.</li>
-    <li id="ac-5" data-criterion>Routing, child/worktree creation, browser/computer/Imagegen work, watcher assignment, route loss, and presentation each read the correct active-harness module before acting; project deltas apply after the global staffing module.</li>
-    <li id="ac-6" data-criterion>Each deferred module is self-contained and contains no runtime branch for the other harness; a missing staffing module blocks dispatch, while a missing presentation module preserves the local artifact and blocks publishing.</li>
-    <li id="ac-7" data-criterion>The compatibility contract records <code>browser-use</code>, <code>computer-use</code>, and <code>imagegen</code> against named harness/tool providers—not model rows—and ranks models only after selecting a reachable provider route.</li>
-    <li id="ac-8" data-criterion>A user-facing image workflow separates Codex-provided generation from human or taste-qualified review; route loss reports an image-generation capability gap rather than substituting browser/computer use.</li>
-    <li id="ac-9" data-criterion>A skill without variants retains the current real <code>.agents</code> primary and expected Claude symlink, with unchanged lock and audit behavior.</li>
-    <li id="ac-10" data-criterion>A declared variant compiles one shared public identity, invocation/execution policy, dependency closure, external contract, and setup owner into correct self-contained real trees for each active harness; overlays that change shared fields are rejected.</li>
-    <li id="ac-11" data-criterion>Variant provenance records the shared source revision plus provider identity and effective-tree hash; install effect-verifies mount type, provider, content, and lock state rather than trusting command exit status.</li>
-    <li id="ac-12" data-criterion>Audit passes valid declared divergence and emits distinct findings for missing provider mount, wrong provider, altered tree/hash, undeclared independent copy, and shared-contract drift.</li>
-    <li id="ac-13" data-criterion>The pilot skill contains zero branches intended only for the other harness and reduces runtime-loaded text by at least 20% in each harness, measured against the current unified package.</li>
-    <li id="ac-14" data-criterion>Migration is module-first, atomic, harness-aware, reversible by proposal rather than deletion, and idempotent: it recognizes and replaces only setup’s seeded legacy header/<code>## Conventions</code> block, refuses an unowned block, preserves unrelated bytes, and a second setup/reconcile makes zero filesystem or lock changes.</li>
-    <li id="ac-15" data-criterion>The provider registry records Codex ChatGPT-in-Chrome as the primary browser route, project-recorded <code>agent-browser</code> as its fallback, Codex Computer Use with no substitute, and native Codex Imagegen with repo <code>codex-imagegen</code> as the headless fallback.</li>
-    <li id="ac-16" data-criterion>Claude Code records no native provider for these three capabilities; any Claude-initiated browser or image work names the machine fallback or dispatched Codex provider explicitly, and never attributes the effect to Claude.</li>
-    <li id="ac-17" data-criterion>The applied contents of the six effective machine files are byte-identical to the complete payloads in this plan, with the two presentation modules intentionally identical.</li>
-    <li id="ac-18" data-criterion>Application begins a fresh transaction barrier, writes and verifies all four deferred modules, preflights both global files before reconciling either, applies both Presentation sections before either Staffing section, verifies all four final sections, and removes the barrier. A stale or partial run cannot satisfy the gate; preflight failure preserves both working inline policies.</li>
-    <li id="ac-19" data-criterion>The Codex-effective roster includes effect-verified Claude worker rows, keeps bounded CLI workers out of coordinator eligibility, maps roster display names to probed CLI aliases, and requires a watched native wrapper for every Codex→Claude invocation.</li>
-    <li id="ac-20" data-criterion>Every cross-harness dispatch runs inside a watched native wrapper whose name identifies the external harness/model and task, whose native staffing is the cheapest model allowed by the current floor, and whose role is limited to process supervision and raw relay. The native spawn seam must accept the resolved wrapper model or report the assigned model; otherwise floor/cost compliance remains unproven.</li>
-  </ul>
